l law displaces the privilege and requires a court to review sensitive information.\n  - id: 19-351, name: Federal Republic of Germany v. Philipp, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether a U.S. court can hear claims against foreign sovereigns for human rights and law of war violations, or if the expropriation exception only applies to property claims.\n  - id: 21-1496, name: Twitter, Inc. v. Taamneh, description: Twitter, Inc. is suing over a law that allows U.S. citizens injured by terrorism to sue companies that knowingly helped terrorists, but the company claims it did not knowingly aid ISIS and only enforced policies against terrorist activity.\n  - id: 20-827, name: United States v. Zubaydah, description: The case United States v. Zubaydah concerns the government\'s obligation to disclose information about a detainee\'s treatment at a secret site in Poland, with the government arguing that certain details can be kept classified.\n  - id: 24-624, name: Case v. Montana, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether Montana\'s police can enter a home without permission, a warrant, or probable cause to help someone in an emergency, and whether the standard for such entries should be more than just a reasonable suspicion.\n  - id: 19-161, name: Department of Homeland Security v. Thuraissigiam, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether a federal statute eliminates the courts\' ability to review deportation decisions, potentially allowing millions of non-citizens to be summarily expelled without judicial oversight.\n  - id: 24-20, name: Fuld v. PLO, description: The case Fuld v. PLO concerns whether the federal government can bring a civil action against organizations responsible for terror activity abroad that kills American citizens, despite their claims that it violates due process rights.\n  - id: 18-776, name: Guerrero-Lasprilla v. Barr, description: The case concerns the scope of judicial review over questions of law decided by the Board of Immigration Appeals, specifically whether courts can review whether the government used the proper legal standard in making decisions.\n  - id: 22-915, name: United States v. Rahimi, description: The case concerns a federal law that temporarily disarms individuals subject to domestic violence protective orders, and whether it violates the Second Amendment right to bear arms.\n  - id: 19-547, name: United States Fish and Wildlife Serv. v. Sierra Club, Inc., description: The Supreme Court is considering whether the government must disclose internal documents related to a decision made in 2014 about the impact of a proposed action on endangered species. The documents were written before the final decision was made and may not have been adopted by the decision-makers.\n  - id: 23-21, name: Harrow v. Dept. of Defense, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a specific filing deadline in the Harrow v. Dept. of Defense case is a procedural rule or a requirement for the court\'s jurisdiction, with implications for how appeals are handled.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
